WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:01.960
MAX SALTONSTALL: Welcome to
"BeyondCorp in a Bottle,"

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.627
where we explore
journeys into the world

00:00:03.627 --> 00:00:05.750
of context-aware access
and zero-trust networks.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:16.070
Perimeter security definitely
was a good idea a while back.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.190
When IT systems just began to
get connected to the internet,

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.350
some sort of barrier had
to protect your systems

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:24.690
on the inside from the
baddies on the outside.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.750
Now this model is broken.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.280
Walls don't work.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.330
And to change, you're going
to need more and new hardware

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.880
or software.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.100
You need to change how you
think about access and security.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.070
While it feels secure, the
perimeter model functions

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.430
more like an egg--

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.470
hard shell on the outside,
soft, gooey center inside.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.600
That's because once
people get past your wall,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.390
they have full access to all
the internal systems you're

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.770
trying to protect.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.190
Looks delicious to an attacker.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:58.070
Add to that your workforce,
heading outside your perimeter

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:00.710
to meetings,
conferences, and clients,

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.600
using mobile devices
along the way, which

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.700
aren't friendly with
your VPN software.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.390
You also want to secure access
for contractors and vendors.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:09.765
But they shouldn't
be able to see

00:01:09.765 --> 00:01:12.330
all of your internal
systems and data.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.360
So you need a way to give
them a limited view--

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.795
a small slice of access.

00:01:16.795 --> 00:01:18.170
As your company
keeps innovating,

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:21.620
you're probably using more
and more cloud services.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.560
The tools have
left the perimeter.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:27.920
Now they're running as a service
on one cloud or another--

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.850
harder and harder for a wall
to keep them all safe inside.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:33.740
And your employees
face constantly

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.020
evolving cyber threats looking
to get inside your perimeter

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.140
through malware, phishing,
hardware exploits, and more.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.080
And it just takes
one Trojan horse

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.860
to bring that adversary
inside your castle.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.530
It's time to change
your security posture.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.780
Enforce strong
multi-factor authentication

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.720
with phishing-resistant
keys, like these.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.150
Get your employee devices
under active management,

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.880
and regularly
monitor their health.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.850
Control access to systems
based on identity and device

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:05.000
data, rather than
location or network.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.110
You can do this today.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:07.720
And I'm here to help.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.340
To get more detail on
the right next steps,

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.583
read more of my
post linked below.

00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:14.000
Thanks for watching,
and subscribe

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.580
to get more videos on
context-aware access.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.610
When it comes to protecting your
company, keep your data yours.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:23.560
